15:15:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The short answer is: **the internet doesn’t break, but it gets more complicated, expensive, and less flexible.** We’ve actually already “run out” in the sense that the global pool of fresh, unassigned IPv4 addresses is empty. Here’s what happens in practice. ### 1. We’ve been running out for years — it’s a gradual squeeze - **February 2011:** The Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA) gave out its last five /8 blocks to the five Regional Internet Registries (RIRs). - **By the 2010s/early 2020s:** Most RIRs announced they had no more unused IPv4 space left to hand out under normal rules. The Asia-Pacific, Europe, North America, and Latin America are all essentially “exhausted.” So we’re already living in the post‑IPv4‑exhaustion world. ### 2. What actually happens to everyday users and businesses? #### a) Your ISP might put you behind *Carrier‑Grade NAT (CGNAT)* Instead of giving your home connection its own unique public IPv4 address, ISPs often place many customers behind one shared public address (just like your home router does for your devices). This works for ordinary web browsing but causes problems: - You can’t easily host a game server, use some peer‑to‑peer apps, or access your security cameras remotely. - Port forwarding stops working in the traditional sense. - Some online gaming and voice/video calls suffer extra latency or connectivity issues. **To a typical user this is invisible most of the time** — Netflix, Facebook, email all work fine because they just use HTTP/HTTPS and initiate connections outwards. #### b) IPv4 addresses become a scarce, tradable commodity - Companies that need public IPv4 addresses (cloud providers, large enterprises, hosting companies) now **buy them on a secondary market** for $20–$60 per address, up from almost nothing years ago. - Startups and smaller players may face higher operational costs or simply be unable to get clean public IP space, forcing them to rely entirely on NAT or jump through technical hoops. #### c) New services may launch **IPv6‑only** (with translation to reach the IPv4 world) Mobile networks and some cloud providers (e.g., T‑Mobile US, some Amazon AWS regions, Facebook’s internal datacenters) have moved to IPv6‑only with translation gateways: - **NAT64 / DNS64** – lets IPv6‑only devices reach IPv4 servers. - **464XLAT** – used on mobile phones to allow apps that only speak IPv4 to work over an IPv6‑only network. This keeps the internet running, but it adds layers of complexity and potential points of failure. ### 3. The “zombie” IPv4 Internet – ever‑growing but stuck Because IPv4 addresses can’t be created, growth comes from reusing existing ones, not from new supply. That means: - **More sharing, more NAT boxes** deep inside ISPs. - **End-to-end connectivity suffers** – the original internet idea of every device being directly reachable is mostly gone for IPv4. - **Increasingly fragile workarounds** – enterprise networks deal with overlapping address spaces, complex VPNs, and layers of address translation. ### 4. What does NOT happen - There is **no “digital blackout”** or sudden stop. Exhaustion was designed into the system; the internet engineering community prepared for it with NAT, CIDR, and IPv6. - Private RFC 1918 addresses (like 192.168.x.x) are unlimited and still work perfectly behind NAT. - The web, email, streaming – all the major services – will keep working, just with more plumbing behind the scenes. ### 5. The real solution: IPv6 IPv6 offers an astronomically large address space (340 undecillion addresses) and restores true end‑to‑end connectivity. In an ideal world, everyone would have adopted IPv6 by now and the IPv4 run‑out would be irrelevant. But adoption, while steadily growing (now ~40–50% of global traffic), is far from 100%. - Many ISPs, mobile carriers, and content giants (Google, YouTube, Netflix, Facebook) already support IPv6. - The long tail of home routers, older devices, and small networks are lagging. **Bottom line:** Running out of IPv4 addresses is like a city running out of new building land — existing buildings still stand, but you can’t build new ones without buying someone else’s lot, building upward (sharing), or moving to a completely new district (IPv6). The internet continues to work, but it’s becoming less flexible, more expensive to expand, and increasingly dependent on clever but intricate workarounds.
